====================================================================================================
CORRECTION NOTICE -- READ BEFORE ANY NUMBER BELOW
====================================================================================================
  The four low-b cutoff values this script used until 2026-07-30 --
    {3.7: 15.0, 3.35: 17.0, 2.85: 22.0, 2.3: 24.0} km/s, +/- 2.0 km/s --
  were labelled 'web-verified, NOT quoted from memory'. They are UNSOURCEABLE. Verification
  against the primary PDFs found: Schaye et al. 2000 (MNRAS 318, 817; astro-ph/9912432) has
  NO table of fitted cutoff values at all, and the 16/22 that sequence resembles are the
  y-AXIS TICKS of its Fig. 4 (simulation model L1); 15 and 24 lie outside that range. Worse,
  the low-z half is REFUTED: Rudie, Steidel & Pettini 2012 (ApJ 757, L30; arXiv:1209.0005)
  Table 2 measures 18.50 +/- 0.22 km/s at <z> = 2.3 at the same pivot, so 24 km/s is 25
  sigma high, and 22-24 km/s would need T0 = 2.9-3.5e4 K, above all 13 studies in their
  Table 3.  REPLACED BY Hiss et al. 2018, ApJ 865, 42 (arXiv:1710.00700), Table 4, with the
  table's own asymmetric errors plus a separate calibration systematic.
  THE SHAPE WAS ALSO WRONG: the retired sequence rose monotonically toward low z
  (22 -> 24 km/s, z = 2.85 -> 2.30); the measured b0(z) TURNS OVER near z = 2.8 and FALLS to
  18.22 km/s at z = 2.0. Schaye's Fig. 4 documents a rise at FIXED N_HI = 10^13.6 in a
  simulation, but Hiss's b0 sits at a z-DEPENDENT pivot log N_HI,0 = 0.6225(1+z) + 11.1068,
  so they are different statistics and Schaye does not license the retired numbers.
====================================================================================================

