====================================================================================================
S6. THE COUNTERWEIGHT, next to the exclusion -- Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015
====================================================================================================
  Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015, MNRAS 448, 2275 (arXiv:1411.2600) simulated the
  Lyman-alpha forest in f(R) gravity and found 'Even models with strong modifications of
  gravity, like |f_R0| = 1e-4, do not change the statistical Lyman-alpha properties by more
  than 10%. The column density and line width distributions are hardly affected at all.'
  Only |f_R0| = 1e-4 and 1e-5 were run (NOT 1e-6), and the Voigt-fit line-width null is
  shown for 1e-5 only. Their matter P(k) does change appreciably, ~15-17% at z = 2 near
  k ~ 30-100 h/Mpc -- which is directly relevant HERE, because S3-S4 of this script build
  <g^2> from exactly that P(k). Note their ~30% temperature figure is Arnold et al. 2014 for
  gas in COLLAPSED objects, not a forest result.

  FOR the framework: the line-width PDF is a velocity-sensitive statistic (their forward
  model carries peculiar velocities and Doppler shifts), and it came out NULL under a strong
  gravity modification. So a large predicted b signature is specific to the POINTWISE
  reading, not generic to modified dynamics. They also document that the forest is
  systematics-limited at ~10% for ANY model -- GR itself misses Kim et al. 2007 at z = 3 by
  more than the error bars (continuum placement, unmodelled low-density IGM heating). That
  bears directly on the calibration channel used above.

  AGAINST reading it as reassurance: f(R) modifies the Poisson SOURCE with a Yukawa-ranged
  additive force capped at G_eff/G = 4/3 (+33%) even fully unscreened (their eqs. 9-10),
  while MI modifies the velocity RESPONSE of unscreened low-acceleration gas with no cap and
  no Yukawa cutoff. A null measured at a 1.33x force change does not bound a ~10x response
  change. And 'screening explains it' is the weakest available reason -- forest gas at
  delta ~ 0-10 is largely unscreened; the null comes from the +33% ceiling, the scalar's
  finite Compton range, and mean-transmission tuning.

  THE BRANCH FORK, computed on this script's own cosmology, both footings:
   branch                             y = a/a0     nu - 1
   LOCAL x_rms (this script) z=2.0      0.0449       382%
   LOCAL x_rms (this script) z=2.8      0.0561       334%
   LOCAL x_rms (this script) z=3.4      0.0644       307%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 canonical z=2            21.21      2.33%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 canonical z=3            31.94      1.55%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 alt z=2                  17.56      2.81%
   EFE cH(z)/a0 alt z=3                  26.45      1.87%
  So: on the LOCAL branch the framework predicts an O(10x) line-width effect that Arnold
  et al. cannot speak to; on the EFE branch it predicts nu - 1 = 1.6-2.8%, BELOW
  f(R)'s <10%, and their null transfers a fortiori. The framework has not closed which field
  enters nu cosmologically, and this script tests only the LOCAL branch. Saying so is
  mandatory: citing Arnold et al. as reassurance silently picks the EFE branch.
  [OK] the EFE branch gives nu - 1 <= 2.8% across both footings, inside f(R)'s <10% band, so the counterweight is real on that branch and this script's exclusion is branch-specific

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  0. THE DATA UNDERNEATH THIS SCRIPT WAS WRONG AND IS NOW SOURCED. The four cutoff values
     and the +/- 2.0 km/s bar are withdrawn as unsourceable (Schaye 2000 has no such table;
     Rudie 2012 measures 18.50 +/- 0.22 km/s at <z> = 2.3, refuting the claimed 24 km/s at
     25 sigma). Replaced by Hiss et al. 2018 Table 4, eight bins, real asymmetric errors.
  1. THE ESCAPE STILL PARTIALLY WORKS. Computing the TOTAL acceleration from the real P(k)
     raises forest gas from x = 0.004-0.013 (single-absorber) to x = 0.045-0.064, a factor
     5.1-11.2, and the exclusion drops from 34-180 sigma statistical to 1.1-9.0 sigma
     (0.4-0.9 sigma on the conservative calibration channel). That is real and it
     matters, and it is the same correction as before -- only the numbers move.
  2. BUT IT DOES NOT CLOSE THE PROBLEM, AND THE SHAPE OF WHAT SURVIVES HAS CHANGED. The
     previous run concluded the constraint was 'carried by z ~ 2.3-2.9 and DIES at z = 3.7'.
     With the real sequence there is no z = 3.7 bin at all, and the surviving significance
     is spread differently: it is largest where Hiss's error bar is smallest (z = 3.0,
     +0.33 km/s) rather than at the low-z end. Weakest bin on the conservative channel:
     z = 2.2 at 0.4 sigma; bins below 3 sigma there: [2.0, 2.2, 2.4, 2.6, 2.8, 3.0, 3.2, 3.4]
     Any banked statement of the form 'dies at z = 3.7' is withdrawn as resting on an
     unsourced datum, NOT softened into a weaker version of itself.
  3. THE UV RISK I FLAGGED DID NOT MATERIALISE, and that is why the answer is trustworthy:
     93% of INT P dk comes from k < 1 Mpc^-1, integrand peaking at lambda ~ 130 Mpc. So
     <g^2> is a LARGE-SCALE quantity, determined rather than cutoff-dependent. Diffuse gas
     genuinely is not rescued by unresolved small-scale structure.
  4. IT CORRECTS THE REASONING BEHIND MY CHANNEL-A FIX. I said matter 'feels its own halo'.
     For BOUND matter that is right -- but because it is bound and has a large LOCAL
     acceleration, NOT because the cosmological field is small-scale dominated (S3 shows it
     is not). Forest gas is unbound, so gets no such boost.
  5. VALIDATED BEFORE USE: v_rms(1D, z=0) = 310 km/s vs the observed ~300 km/s.

  ONE DIRECTION LEFT UNPRICED HERE, and it runs the framework's way: the rms is a VOLUME
  average over random positions, while forest absorbers sit at mildly OVERDENSE positions.
  mi_forest_conditional_accel_2026 computes that conditional distribution directly.

  STATUS: the diffuse-sector constraint on the POINTWISE branch is REAL and now rests on a
  sourced table -- 1-9 sigma statistical, 0.4-0.9 sigma against the method
  systematic, across all eight Hiss bins and both a0 footings. Two caveats stand: the b
  budget is LCDM-hydro-calibrated (and Arnold et al. show the forest is systematics-limited
  at ~10% for everyone), and the EFE-vs-local branch fork is unclosed, with the EFE branch
  predicting an effect BELOW f(R)'s and therefore untestable here. Enough to keep the
  missing regulator MANDATORY on the pointwise branch; not a statement about the framework.
====================================================================================================
